    "summary": "Stellar Truckin' & Let It Grow; Keith's organ shines",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a soaring Truckin' into an expansive Let It Grow, highlighted by Keith Godchaux’s unusually forward organ work. Jerry’s leads are inspired, Phil provides solid bass, and Bill’s drums dominate the extended jam. The encore was cut short when an audience fight interrupted Casey Jones.",
    "review": "On October 23, 1973 at the Metropolitan Sports Center in Bloomington, the Dead rolled out a two‑set marathon that still feels fresh for Deadheads. Set 2 is where the night truly shines – a ripping Truckin' segues into a Nobody's Fault But Mine jam and then bursts into an unbridled The Other One before Bill Kreutzmann launches his Drums segment. From there the band weaves through Weather Report Suite Prelude, Part 1 and lets Let It Grow blossom, with Keith Godchaux’s organ thrust forward in the mix, delivering a solo that steals the show. Jerry Garcia’s leads are extra‑inspired on both Truckin' and Let It Grow, while Phil Lesh holds down a driving bass line despite his visible frustration during the chaotic Casey Jones finale, which was abruptly halted by an audience fight. Bob Weir’s vocal energy is evident on Mexicali Blues, even if his guitar sits a bit high in the mix. The soundboard capture (gd1973-10-23.sbd.miller.92792.sbeok.flac16) presents Keith’s organ front‑and‑center, clear overall tones, and only minor level‑adjustment quirks early on, making it the definitive listening source for this memorable night.",
